in": 17.04, "prior_sd": 5.34, "poll_margin": 19.57, "poll_sd": 6.96, "poll_weight": 0.37, "fundamentals": {"margin": 18.6, "lean": 10.5, "environment": 4.1, "incumbency": 4.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 16.0, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.4, "baseline_margin": 17.98, "analyst_adjustment": 0.02, "key_factors": ["Healey leads every 2026 poll by 16 to 24 points, averaging about 20", "D+14 state with a well-known incumbent seeking reelection", "All seven expert ratings place the race at Solid or Safe D", "Prediction markets price Healey at roughly 95 percent to win"], "rationale": "Polls throughout 2026 show Healey ahead of Minogue by double digits, every major rater calls the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and markets give Minogue little chance; the mid-October debates produced no sign of movement in a race this one-sided.", "watch": "A major late controversy or an unusual turnout swing could narrow a margin this large, but no current evidence points in that direction.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Maura Healey (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Maura Healey seeks a second term in a state with a fourteen-point Democratic partisan lean. Polling throughout 2026 has shown her with wide leads over Republican Mike Minogue, with the most recent September surveys giving her margins of sixteen to twenty-one points among likely voters. Every major election rater, including the Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, and Sabato's Crystal Ball, places the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column. Betting markets similarly price Healey's chances at roughly ninety-five percent or better.\n\nThe final debates in mid-October featured sharp exchanges on energy costs, immigration, and Trump-related issues, along with post-debate fact-checking, but nothing suggests they altered the contest. Minogue, a first-time statewide candidate, faces an incumbent with strong name recognition and a consistent polling advantage. Independent candidate Andrea James 